EntityManager - INTERFACE used to make a new entity persistent and managed
resulting in its insertion in database when the state of the object changes upon synchronization with db

Object obj = repos.findbyid(objId). orElseThrow(): - a managed object using JPA

objEntitymanager.flush() - forces all pending sql statements to execute but does not committ the chnages

objEntityManager.detach(obj) - makes JPA to stop tracking the changes from here on unless invoked again with say .save()

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Lazy Fecthing - don't retrieve the data until the application actually needs them
say we have a many to one relationship
1 customer - many orders

when we want to get customer's list of orders
e.g customer = repo.findall()
JPA will first run 1 query to get that customers

then since the fecth type is lazy it will run seperate queries to fecth for each record
if we need to get their orders JPA will run select orders ... where id ... for each customer in a loop

so if there are 5 customers
1 query for all customers , then 1 query each for all 5 customers orders
hence 1 + N  called the N+1 problem

this can cause performance issues especially with large data


possible solutions to N+1 problem
- Using Distinct and join queries to run 1 query instead of multiple queries
in the jpa repo
using @Query annotation
e.g.
@Query("SELECT DISTINCT c FROM Customer c LEFT JOIN FETCH c.orders")
List<Orders> findAllOrders();

++++++++++++++++++++

another solution is @EntityGraph
@EntityGraph has attribute so that JPA resolves the join internally itself to find that attribute

e.g.
@EntityGraph(attributePaths = "orders")
@Query(SELECT c FROM Customer c)
List<Customers> getCustomerOrders();

Note: JPQL works with entities not db tables